{"name":"Barbarossa","hex":"#a84734","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Barbarossa","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Italian.","An epithet or nickname applied to certain historical characters."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Barbarossa"}],"entries":[{"title":"Frederick Barbarossa","blocks":[{"text":"Frederick Barbarossa, also known as Frederick I, was the Holy Roman Emperor from 1155 until his death in 1190. He was elected King of Germany in Frankfurt on 4 March 1152 and crowned in Aachen on 9 March 1152. He was crowned King of Italy on 24 April 1155 in Pavia and emperor by Pope Adrian IV on 18 June 1155 in Rome. Two years later, the term sacrum (\"holy\") first appeared in a document in connection with his empire. He was later formally crowned King of Burgundy, at Arles on 30 June 1178. His nickname of Barbarossa \"was first used by the Florentines only in 1298 to differentiate the emperor from his grandson, Frederick II ... and was never employed in medieval Germany\". In German, he was known as Kaiser Rotbart, which in English means \"Emperor Redbeard\". The prevalence of the Italian nickname, even in later German usage, reflects the centrality of the Italian campaigns under his reign, and \"remains to this day one of the [most] powerful historical monikers.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg/330px-Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_Barbarossa","wikidata":"Q79789"},{"title":"Hayreddin Barbarossa","blocks":[{"text":"Hayreddin Barbarossa, also known as Hayreddin Pasha, Hızır Hayrettin Pasha, and simply Hızır Reis, was an Ottoman corsair and later admiral of the Ottoman Navy. Barbarossa's naval victories secured Ottoman dominance over the Mediterranean during the mid-16th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Hayreddin_-_Algeria_Fine_Art_Museum.jpg/330px-Hayreddin_-_Algeria_Fine_Art_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayreddin_Barbarossa","wikidata":"Q200582"},{"title":"Barbarossa (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Barbarossa is a 2009 Italian English-language epic historical drama film set primarily in northern Italy during the late 12th century. Despite the film's title, Friedrich \"Barbarossa\" features only as a supporting character in this film, which is primarily concerned with the struggle of the Lombard League, which struggled to maintain independence from the Holy Roman Empire, led by the legendary Guelph warrior Alberto da Giussano."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Barbarossa_film_-_locandina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbarossa_(film)","wikidata":"Q807770"},{"title":"Barbarossa (musician)","blocks":[{"text":"James Mathé, known professionally as Barbarossa, is a British singer-songwriter, producer, and musician known for his minimalist electronic music. Formerly part of the Fence Collective, Mathé first performed as Barbarossa in 2005, releasing his debut EP Sea Like Blood the same year. He is currently signed under the label Memphis Industries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Barbarossa_in_2006.jpg/330px-Barbarossa_in_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbarossa_(musician)","wikidata":"Q60735562"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg/500px-Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg/1280px-Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg/500px-Crusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d%27Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_%28late_15th_C%29%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Crusaders besieging Damascus in 1148","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrusaders_besieging_Damascus_-_Chronique_d'Ernoul_et_de_Bernard_le_Tr%C3%A9sorier_(late_15th_C)%2C_f.280v_-_BL_Royal_MS_15_E_I.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG/500px-StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG/1280px-StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG/500px-StrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"13th-century stained glass image of Frederick I, Strasbourg Cathedral","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStrasbourgCath_BasCoteN_08.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Keizer_Frederik_I